Issue: The harness answers with whatever single reply the model happens to produce, and on the questions where the model's answer distribution is wide that one reply is close to a coin flip. Replaying every raw reply of the four recorded runs shows how wide: on USPTO the two samples of the same question disagree on 23 of 30 examples (identical-prediction 7/30), and the parent run's 60 USPTO samples hold only 10 correct answers. Pooling the 8 independent replies every example has across the four runs, one reply scores USPTO 0.154 / Symptom2Disease 0.845 / LawBench 0.560, while the answer a panel of those same replies agrees on scores 0.229 / 0.847 / 0.565 (k=5, 200 random partitions). Every other lever was already measured dead: memory-side repairs are exhausted (103 of the 170 residual LawBench failures are themselves demonstrated charge names and only 8 sit at edit distance 2 from one), the retrieved context does not steer the answer (the predicted charge is visible in it for 62% of the subset failures and the missing one for 58% — a comparison matched inside the same case), and no chemistry toolkit is importable for USPTO notation.

Modification: `solver/agent.py` now asks the solver several times per sample and answers with the reply the panel agrees on (`PANEL_SIZE = 5`, `PANEL_MINIMUM = 3`, `PANEL_BUDGET = 0.5`): every attempt is an independent agent run on a fresh copy of the inputs, and every attempt's reply is grounded in the memory's answer space before it is counted, so the near misses the grounding canonicalises vote together. The panel stops as soon as three replies already agree, stops starting attempts once half of the sample's own time budget (`config["timeout_seconds"]`) is spent, and still answers with the replies it has when an attempt dies on the model-call limit or the deadline. `solver/answer_space.py` gains the two comparisons this needs: `answer_text` (the answer a reply carries) and `panel_winner` / `answers_agree` (the most common answer under the memory's own annotation rules, the earliest reply breaking a tie).

